 Microbiol. Cell.</source><volume>25</volume><year>1976</year><fpage>97</fpage><lpage>106</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="Zecevicetal1998"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zecevic</surname><given-names>M.</given-names></name><name><surname>Catling</surname><given-names>A.D.</given-names></name><name><surname>Eblen</surname><given-names>S.T.</given-names></name><name><surname>Renzi</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Hittle</surname><given-names>J.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Yen</surname><given-names>T.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Gorbsky</surname><given-names>G.J.</given-names></name><name><surname>Weber</surname><given-names>M.J.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Active MAP kinase in mitosislocalization at kinetochores and association with the motor protein CENP-E</article-title><source>J. Cell Biol</source><volume>142</volume><year>1998</year><fpage>1547</fpage><lpage>1558</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9744883</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn><p>The online version of this article contains supplemental material.</p></fn><fn><p>Dr. Yucel's current address is Genomica, 1745 38th St., Boulder, CO 80301.</p></fn><fn><p>Dr. Philp's current address is CEST, 5 Berners Road, Islington, London N1 0PW, UK.</p></fn><fn><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> aa, amino acids&#x0003b; nt(s), nucleotide(s)&#x0003b; utr, untranslated region.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The <italic>CENP-meta</italic> locus in <italic>D</italic>. <italic>melanogaster</italic>. (A) Sequence analysis of 24 kb of the 32D-E region of chromosome 2 revealed the presence of five genes encoding two new kinesin related proteins, <italic>CENP-meta</italic> and <italic>CENP-ana</italic>, a putative palmitoyl protein thioesterase (PPT-2), an exo/endonuclease (MRE 11), and the 3&#x02032; untranslated region of the <italic>abrupt</italic> gene. Exon structure, sites of P-element insertions, and direction of transcription (arrows) are shown as well as a schematic representation of both the <italic>CENP-meta</italic> cDNA and encoded protein. In addition, the location of the probe used for RNA blot analysis, the location of the peptide used to generate an antibody to CENP<italic>-</italic>meta, a protein expression construct used for antibody purification, and a subset of cDNA clones isolated for <italic>CENP-meta</italic> are indicated. The deduced amino acid sequence of <italic>CENP-meta</italic> is available from EMBL/GenBank/BDGP accession number AF220353. (B) Domain comparisons of CENP-meta, CENP-ana, HCENP-E and XCENP-E. Length in amino acids is indicated for each domain. (C) Sequence comparison indicating the percentage of identity between species for each domain. Human CENP-E (HCENP-E)&#x0003b; <italic>Xenopus</italic> CENP-E (XCENP-E).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0003140.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>CENP<italic>-</italic>meta localizes to kinetochores at all stages of the cell cycle. (A) Localization of CENP<italic>-</italic>meta in cultured <italic>Drosophila</italic> S2 cells. Cycling S2 cells were fixed and stained with mouse monoclonal anti-alpha tubulin antibody (green), affinity-purified rabbit anti-CENP<italic>-</italic>meta antibody (red), and chromatin was visualized by staining with Hoechst 33342 (blue). Cells at various stages of the cell cycle are shown as deconvolved stack projections (four small images on left) or as a regular fluorescence micrograph (large metaphase image at right). I, interphase&#x0003b; M, metaphase&#x0003b; A, anaphase&#x0003b; T, telophase. (B) CENP<italic>-</italic>meta is present on the kinetochores in syncytial embryos. Embryos were fixed and stained to reveal DNA (DAPI, blue), microtubules (using YL1/2, green) and CENP<italic>-</italic>meta (no. 6584, red). (C) Polyclonal antibody to CENP<italic>-</italic>meta characterized by immunoprecipitation and immunoblotting. Immunoprecipitations of CENP<italic>-</italic>meta from total S2 cellular lysates using preimmune sera (lane 1), or affinity-purified CENP<italic>-</italic>meta antibody (lane 2) followed by immunoblotting with the affinity-purified CENP<italic>-</italic>meta antibody. The full-length protein, indicated by an asterisk, is always a small fraction of the total protein recovered. The majority of the immunoreactive protein is present as a 180-kD band, presumably due to protein degradation. (Lane 3) CENP<italic>-</italic>meta in S2 whole cell lysate. (D) CENP<italic>-</italic>meta is found at kinetochores of isolated mitotic chromosomes. (Red) CENP<italic>-</italic>meta is associated with the primary constrictions of all chromosomes&#x0003b; (blue) chromosomes stained with DAPI. Bars, 5 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0003140.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>RNA blotting reveals <italic>CENP-meta<sup>&#x00394;</sup></italic> to be a null mutant of <italic>CENP-meta</italic>. Total RNA was isolated from larvae of various genotypes, separated on denaturing formaldehyde gels, transferred to membranes, probed for the <italic>CENP-meta</italic> transcript (top row) and then sequentially stripped and probed for <italic>CENP-ana</italic> (middle row) and the <italic>mcm5</italic> transcript (bottom row, as loading control). Lanes were loaded as follows: 15 &#x003bc;g total RNA from adult females (lane 1), 30 ug total RNA isolated from larvae heterozygous for P-element <italic>l(2)k07716</italic> (lane 2), and larvae homozygous (lane 3) or heterozygous (lane 4) for the <italic>cmet<sup>&#x00394;</sup></italic> derivative of P-element <italic>l(2)04431</italic>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0003140.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Lack of functional <italic>CENP-meta</italic> in embryo clones results in alignment defects. 0&#x02013;2 h wild-type embryos (A and D), embryos derived from germline clones of <italic>cmet<sup>lx1</sup></italic> (B and E) and <italic>cmet<sup>&#x00394;</sup></italic> (C and F) were fixed and stained for DNA (DAPI, red), microtubules (YL1/2, green), and for CP190 (blue), which is a protein associated with nuclei in interphase and centrosomes in mitosis. Many mitotic nuclei (examples arrowed) show chromosomes that are not correctly aligned on the metaphase plate and some centrosomes that are no longer associated with nuclei (yellow arrowheads). In the <italic>cmet<sup>&#x00394;</sup></italic> embryos mitotic nuclei were less frequently observed and CP190 was sometimes associated with condensed chromatin (white arrowheads in C and F) rather than centrosomes (arrowed nucleus in C and F). Bar, 10 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0003140.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Absence of <italic>CENP-meta</italic> delays cells in a prometaphase-like state. Brains from third instar animals of <italic>cmet<sup>lx1</sup></italic> (A and C), <italic>cmet<sup>&#x00394;</sup></italic> (B), and <italic>cmet<sup>lx1</sup></italic>/+ (D) were squashed and stained to reveal chromosomes. The mean total numbers of mitotic cells per field examined and the numbers of cells in different stages of mitosis were counted (E). At least 300 fields were counted. Error bars represent standard error of the means. Whole brains from <italic>cmet<sup>lx1</sup></italic> (F) or <italic>cmet<sup>lx1</sup></italic>/+ third instar larvae (G) were immunostained for DNA (DAPI&#x0003b; red) and microtubules (YL1/2&#x0003b; green). Chromosomes from CENP-meta mutant brains are much less tightly associated with the metaphase plate than in wild-type cells (compare arrowed figures in F and G). Bars, 5 &#x003bc;m.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0003140.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><italic>cmet<sup>lx1</sup></italic> embryos cannot maintain metaphase chromosome alignment. Embryos carrying <italic>cmet<sup>lx1</sup></italic>(A&#x02013;C) as their only copy of <italic>CENP-meta</italic> or the starting histone-2A-variant-GFP stock (D) carrying a wild-type <italic>CENP-meta</italic> allele were collected and observed in real-time. Chromosomes are visible due to incorporation of histone-2A-variant-GFP into all chromosomes. (A) A misaligned chromosome (pseudo-colored blue) initially moves toward one pole in anaphase, but upon chromatid disjunction one sister reverses and ultimately segregates to the opposite pole. (B) Both chromatids of a pair (red) that fails to maintain a congressed state initially, move together toward the top pole but at anaphase onset (synchronous for all chromosomes in this nucleus) the chromatids disjoin and one chromatid moves towards the lower pole. (C) A series of different chromatid pairs (green, red, and orange) fail to maintain a tightly congressed chromosome alignment and do not disjoin. (D) A normal congression, maintenance of alignment and anaphase in a wild-type embryo. Numbers indicate relative elapsed time in seconds&#x0003b; yellow dotted lines in 0 time panels indicate approximate direction of spindle axis in nucleus being displayed. Each panel is a 20-&#x003bc;m square. Videos 1 and 2, further depicting <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">Fig. 6A&#x02013;C</xref> and <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F6">Fig. D</xref>, respectively, are available at http://www.jcb.org/cgi/content/full/150/1/1/DC1.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0003140.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p><italic>cmet<sup>&#x00394;</sup></italic> embryos cannot maintain metaphase chromosome alignment. Embryos carrying <italic>cmet<sup>&#x00394;</sup></italic> as the only copy of <italic>CENP-meta</italic> were collected and observed in real-time. Chromosomes are visible due to incorporation of histone-2A-variant-GFP. Chromosomes (pseudo-colored red in A, green in B, and orange in C) lose initial alignment, becoming chronically misaligned. Most misaligned chromosomes do not disjoin into separate chromatids and both sisters segregate together to the same pole in anaphase. Numbers indicate relative elapsed time in seconds&#x0003b; yellow dotted lines in 0 time panels indicate approximate direction of spindle axis in nucleus being displayed. Each panel is a 20-&#x003bc;m square. Video 3 is available at http://www.jcb.org/cgi/content/full/150/1/1/DC1.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JCB0003140.f7"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>